--TEST--
`Core\Router::url` and `Core\Router::urlAbsolute` throw on a method reference held in a value whose method has no route, and refuse exactly the same references as each other — `rule:routing/link-target-and-params-are-checked`, `rule:routing/an-absolute-link-takes-a-configured-origin`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The depth claim is *agreement*: one question asked of the two members that
// share one lookup, asserting that they answer the same rather than what each
// answered. `urlAbsolute` is `url` with `rule:routing/an-absolute-link-takes-a-configured-origin`'s configured origin
// in front, so a reference one of them resolves and the other does not would
// mean two lookups had grown apart — which is exactly what a second copy of the
// table would produce.
//
// The references are held in values rather than written at the call, because
// a *written* reference to a method with no route is a compile error and a
// value reference is a throw. This case is about the throw.
//
// This program declares no `#[Route]` and no `autoload` root, so the table it
// asks is empty by construction (`rule:routing/table-is-opt-in` — a program that
// declares no route still performs no scan), and every method below has no row.
class Users {
    public static function show(): string { return "show"; }
    public static function index(): string { return "index"; }
    public function edit(): string { return "edit"; }
}

var $users = new Users();
array<callable> $targets = [
    Users::show,
    Users::index,
    // An instance method, bound to its object, which has no route either.
    $users->edit(...),
];

var $agree = 0;
foreach ($targets as callable $target) {
    var $path = "resolved";
    try {
        Core\Router::url($target);
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        $path = "threw";
    }

    var $absolute = "resolved";
    try {
        Core\Router::urlAbsolute($target);
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        $absolute = "threw";
    }

    if ($path == $absolute) {
        $agree = $agree + 1;
    }
}

// Both halves: the two members agreed on every reference, and what they agreed
// on is the refusal. Counting the agreements rather than printing each answer
// is what makes a member that grew its own lookup fail here.
echo "agree=", $agree as string, "/", Core\Arr::count($targets) as string, "\n";

var $refused = 0;
foreach ($targets as callable $target) {
    try {
        Core\Router::url($target);
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        $refused = $refused + 1;
    }
}
echo "refused=", $refused as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
agree=3/3
refused=3
